To generate a table of contents (TOC) automatically in a LaTeX document, you can use the `tocloft` package, which provides more flexibility in customizing the TOC. Here's an example:

1. Add the following lines to the preamble of your LaTeX document:

```latex
\documentclass{article}

\usepackage{tocloft} % for customizing the table of contents

% Set the title of the table of contents
\renewcommand{\contentsname}{Table of Contents}

% Set the font size and spacing of the table of contents
\setlength{\cftfont}{\large}
\setlength{\cfttocskip}{1pc}

% Set the leader character for the table of contents
\renewcommand{\cftdot}{\ensuremath{\bullet}}

% Set the number of digits for the section numbers
\setcounter{tocdepth}{2} % show up to level 2 (i.e., sections and subsections)

\begin{document}
```

2. Add the following command where you want the TOC to appear:

```latex
\tableofcontents
```

3. Add section and subsection headings to your document, and they will automatically appear in the TOC. For example:

```latex
\section{Introduction}
\subsection{Background}
\subsection{Motivation}

\section{Methodology}
\subsection{Data Collection}
\subsection{Data Analysis}

\section{Results}
\subsection{Descriptive Statistics}
\subsection{Hypothesis Testing}

\section{Discussion}
\subsection{Implications}
\subsection{Limitations and Future Work}

\end{document}
```

This will generate a TOC at the beginning of your document, with customizations such as font size, spacing, leader character, and depth level of section headings. Note that the `tocdepth` counter should be set to the desired level of section headings you want to include in the TOC. In this example, it is set to 2, which includes sections and subsections. You can adjust this value to include other levels (e.g., chapters, subsubsections, etc.).

