In Java, the `==` operator compares the references of two objects, not their values. For strings, this means that `==` compares whether two string objects refer to the same instance in memory, rather than comparing their character sequences.

On the other hand, the `.equals()` method is a standard method for string comparison in Java. It compares the character sequences of two string objects, regardless of whether they refer to the same instance or not.

Using `==` to compare strings is generally considered bad practice, as it may lead to unexpected behavior and bugs. The correct way to compare strings in Java is to use the `.equals()` method.

Here's an example to illustrate the difference:

```java
String str1 = "hello";
String str2 = "hello";
String str3 = new String("hello");

// Using the == operator
if (str1 == str2) {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 refer to the same instance");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 refer to different instances");
}

if (str1 == str3) {
    System.out.println("str1 and str3 refer to the same instance");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str3 refer to different instances");
}

// Using the .equals() method
if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 have the same character sequence");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 have different character sequences");
}

if (str1.equals(str3)) {
    System.out.println("str1 and str3 have the same character sequence");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str3 have different character sequences");
}
```

